<PubmedArticle><MedlineCitation Status="MEDLINE" Owner="NLM" IndexingMethod="Manual"><PMID Version="1">8042461</PMID><DateCompleted><Year>1994</Year><Month>08</Month><Day>24</Day></DateCompleted><DateRevised><Year>2019</Year><Month>08</Month><Day>15</Day></DateRevised><Article PubModel="Print"><Journal><ISSN IssnType="Print">0001-6349</ISSN><JournalIssue CitedMedium="Print"><Volume>73</Volume><Issue>6</Issue><PubDate><Year>1994</Year><Month>Jul</Month></PubDate></JournalIssue><Title>Acta obstetricia et gynecologica Scandinavica</Title><ISOAbbreviation>Acta Obstet Gynecol Scand</ISOAbbreviation></Journal><ArticleTitle>Pulsatile gonadotropin releasing hormone (GnRH)--treatment for hypothalamic amenorrhoea causing infertility.</ArticleTitle><Pagination><StartPage>482</StartPage><EndPage>485</EndPage><MedlinePgn>482-5</MedlinePgn></Pagination><Abstract><AbstractText>Thirty women with hypothalamic amenorrhoea and infertility were treated with pulsatile GnRH s.c. without monitoring with estradiol and ultrasonography. Ovulation rate per patient was 93%. Ovulation rate per cycle was 90%. Pregnancy rate per ovulatory cycle 30%. Miscarriage rate 18.5%. 23 children were born including one set of twins. No serious side effects were recorded.</AbstractText></Abstract><AuthorList CompleteYN="Y"><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Skarin</LastName><ForeName>G</ForeName><Initials>G</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Specialist Open-care Unit of Obstetrics and Gynecology, Uppsala, Sweden.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Ahlgren</LastName><ForeName>M</ForeName><Initials>M</Initials></Author></AuthorList><Language>eng</Language><PublicationTypeList><PublicationType UI="D016430">Clinical Trial</PublicationType><PublicationType UI="D016428">Journal Article</PublicationType></PublicationTypeList></Article><MedlineJournalInfo><Country>United States</Country><MedlineTA>Acta Obstet Gynecol Scand</MedlineTA><NlmUniqueID>0370343</NlmUniqueID><ISSNLinking>0